#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065338 :tutorial/content "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^6.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n(5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n\t5^6\n}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "1a2e18f4-6996-52a0-95eb-77abb10d0e77" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1480 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065339 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "40488864-6672-59b7-9ac6-79903856b484" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1481 :tutorial/title "The Power Rule With Fractional Exponents"}]}
